.docname {Package CLI}
.docauthor {BakingRL}
.include {docs}
.doclang {en}

`@bakingrl/create-plugin` publishes two commands:

- `create-bakingrl-plugin`: creates a new plugin package from an official
  template.
- `bakingrl-plugin`: validates, inspects, signs, packs, and installs plugin
  packages.

Generated packages also receive a local helper copy at
`scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs`. Prefer the generated npm scripts inside a plugin
package so every package uses the helper version it was created with.

## Installation

Published install:

```sh
npm install -g @bakingrl/create-plugin
```

Local SDK checkout install:

```sh
cd BakingRLSDK
npm install
npm install -g . --force
```

When installed from a local SDK checkout, generated packages use the local
`packages/plugin-sdk` through a `file:` dependency. When installed from npm,
generated packages use the published SDK range.

## Generator Command

```sh
create-bakingrl-plugin [directory-name] [--template <template-id>] [--force]
```

Arguments and options:

- `directory-name`: target package directory. If omitted, the command prompts
  for it.
- `--template <template-id>` or `-t <template-id>`: template to use. The
  current template is `fullstack-plugin`.
- `--force`: removes an existing target directory before writing the template.
  Use this only when the target can be overwritten.

The generator:

- validates and slugifies the target directory name;
- creates a plugin ID like `com.example.my_package`;
- copies the selected template;
- writes package metadata into `package.json` and `bakingrl.plugin.json`;
- copies the package helper to `scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs`;
- prints the next build, pack, and local install commands.

Example:

```sh
create-bakingrl-plugin rl-scoreboard
cd rl-scoreboard
npm install
npm run build
npm run pack
npm run install:local
```

From the SDK repository without a global install:

```sh
cd BakingRLSDK
npm run create -- ../BakingRLPlugins/rl-scoreboard
```

## Generated NPM Scripts

Generated packages expose these scripts:

```json
{
  "build": "vite build",
  "check": "tsc --noEmit",
  "validate": "node scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs validate",
  "add": "node scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs add",
  "pack": "node scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs pack",
  "keygen": "node scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs keygen",
  "sign": "node scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs sign",
  "inspect": "node scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs inspect",
  "install:local": "node scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs install-local"
}
```

Use `--` when passing helper arguments through npm:

```sh
npm run add -- visual boostMeter
npm run pack -- --sign signing-key.json
npm run sign -- --key signing-key.json
```

## Helper Command Reference

All helper commands accept paths relative to the current working directory.

`validate [package-dir]`

Validates a built package directory. Default directory is `.`.

```sh
npm run validate
node scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs validate ./my-package
bakingrl-plugin validate ./my-package
```

Validation checks:

- `bakingrl.plugin.json` exists and is valid JSON;
- `manifest.schema` is `bakingrl.plugin/2`;
- `id`, `name`, and `version` are non-empty strings;
- `id` uses supported characters, has no empty dot segments, and does not use
  the reserved `plugin.` runtime prefix;
- `compatibility.runtimeApi` exists and targets helper runtime API `0.4.x`;
- package-level `settings`, when declared, points to a JSON Schema object with
  supported primitive fields;
- secret settings use `type: "string"`, `x-bakingrl-secret: true`, and no
  default value;
- `exports` exists and contains at least one capability;
- visual, component, service, and connector entries point to existing built
  `.js` files inside the package;
- page and layout template paths stay inside the package and exist;
- configuration page paths stay inside the package and private configuration
  visual entries point to built `.js` files;
- deprecated `exports.overlays` is not used.

Run `npm run build` before `validate` because validation checks built entry
files.

`add <visual|component|service|connector> <export-name> [package-dir]`

Adds a new capability to an existing generated package.

```sh
npm run add -- visual boostMeter
npm run add -- component PlayerCard
npm run add -- service matchStats
npm run add -- connector twitch
```

`add` updates `bakingrl.plugin.json`, creates the source file, creates a schema
file when needed, and adds a Rollup input to `vite.config.ts`.

Generated export defaults:

- visual: `src/visuals/<slug>/index.ts`,
  `dist/visuals/<slug>.js`, and `defaultSize: [320, 120]`;
- component: `src/components/<slug>/index.ts`,
  `dist/components/<slug>.js`, and `props.schema.json`;
- service: `src/services/<slug>/index.ts`,
  `dist/services/<slug>.js`, `methods: ["ping"]`, and
  `methods.schema.json`;
- connector: `src/connectors/<slug>/index.ts` and
  `dist/connectors/<slug>.js`.

The helper refuses to overwrite existing source or manifest entries.

`inspect [package-dir]`

Validates the package and prints the normalized package ID, version, exports,
imports, and permissions as JSON.

```sh
npm run inspect
bakingrl-plugin inspect ./my-package
```

Use `inspect` before packaging when you want to verify which runtime surface the
host will see.

`pack [package-dir] [--sign <key-file>]`

Validates the package, writes `manifest.hashes.json`, optionally writes
`signature.ed25519`, and creates a `.brlp` archive in `dist-bundles/`.

```sh
npm run pack
npm run pack -- --sign signing-key.json
```

The archive is a ZIP32 bundle. The helper excludes `node_modules`, `.git`, and
`dist-bundles`. If the signing key file is inside the package directory, it is
excluded from the hash list and archive.

`install-local [package-dir]`

Validates the package and copies it to BakingRL's local app-data package
directory.

```sh
npm run install:local
bakingrl-plugin install-local ./my-package
```

Default target directories:

- macOS: `~/Library/Application Support/com.quillianne.bakingrl/packages`
- Windows: `%LOCALAPPDATA%/com.quillianne.bakingrl/packages`
- Linux: `$XDG_DATA_HOME/com.quillianne.bakingrl/packages` or
  `~/.local/share/com.quillianne.bakingrl/packages`

Set `BAKINGRL_PACKAGES_DIR` to override the target during development.

`packages-dir`

Prints the local app-data package directory that `install-local` uses.

```sh
bakingrl-plugin packages-dir
node scripts/bakingrl-plugin.mjs packages-dir
```

`keygen [key-file]`

Creates an Ed25519 signing key file. Default file is
`bakingrl-signing-key.json`.

```sh
npm run keygen -- signing-key.json
```

The helper writes the file with private key material. Do not commit it.

`sign --key <key-file> [package-dir]`

Validates the package, writes `manifest.hashes.json`, and writes
`signature.ed25519`.

```sh
npm run sign -- --key signing-key.json
bakingrl-plugin sign --key signing-key.json ./my-package
```

`sign` does not create a `.brlp` archive. Use `pack --sign <key-file>` when you
want signing and bundling in one command.

## Development Loop

For most local package work:

```sh
npm run build
npm run check
npm run validate
npm run pack
npm run install:local
```

Then reload packages in the BakingRL host application.
